
 fillrdadb - Run the standard data-usage fill pipeline.

 Wrapper that fills usage tables in PostgreSQL database 'rdadb' for the
 selected time range by invoking, in order:
   fillcodusage, fillglobususage, fillawsusage, fillosdfusage.

 USAGE:
   fillrdadb [-b] [-n] [-B NumberDaysBack]
             (-c | -d DateList | -m MonthList | -N NumberDays | -y YearList)
   fillrdadb [-b] [-n] -B NumberDaysBack

 OPTIONS (choose exactly one of -c, -d, -m, -N, or -y, or none with -B):

   -c   Clean unused file records only (do not fill new usages).

   -d DateList
        Process usages recorded on the given dates.
        Date format: YYYY-MM-DD (e.g. 2024-01-15).

   -m MonthList
        Process usages recorded in the given months.
        Month format: YYYY-MM (e.g. 2024-01).

   -N NumberDays
        Process usages recorded in the most recent NumberDays days.

   -y YearList
        Process usages recorded in the given years.
        Year format: YYYY (e.g. 2024).

   -b   Run in background mode (log to file only, no terminal output).

   -n   Do not send email notification when filling is complete.

   -B NumberDaysBack
        After the -N pass, reprocess any date between NumberDaysBack days ago
        and the start of the -N window that still holds no usage record.

        A log file is sometimes published after its date has slid out of the
        -N window, and nothing reaches for it again, so that date stays empty
        however often the job runs. Each usage table is asked which dates it
        is missing, and only those dates are reprocessed, so a late log is
        picked up however late it arrives.

        A date whose log holds no usable entry keeps being offered until it
        ages past NumberDaysBack. That is deliberate: treating an already
        processed log as proof of coverage would silently write off the dates
        left empty by a run that died partway.

        Given on its own, with none of -c, -d, -m, -N or -y, it is taken as
        -N 1, so that the missed dates can be swept without naming a window.
        Given with -c, -d, -m or -y, it is ignored with a warning.

 EXAMPLE:
   fillrdadb -N 7
   fillrdadb -N 14 -B 60     # weekly fill, reaching back two months
   fillrdadb -B 60           # same as -N 1 -B 60: sweep the last two months
